@title Product Design Principles
@subtitle How we design customer-facing apps at Fathom Bank
@author Design Systems team, Fathom Bank

These principles were agreed in a workshop with 26 designers and engineers on 2026-09-17. They apply to all customer-facing apps, and every design review should check work against them.

# Clarity over cleverness
Customers come to us to manage their money, not to decode an interface. When a plain label and a clever one compete, the plain one wins, especially where money moves.
- **Do**: label buttons with the action.
- **Avoid**: icons without text for money movement.

# Show the money
Customers should never be surprised by what something costs. Every fee, rate and total belongs on screen early enough to change their mind.
- **Do**: show fees before confirmation.
- **Avoid**: revealing fees on the final screen.

# Forgive mistakes
People tap the wrong thing, pick the wrong account and change their minds. Our flows should make recovering from a mistake as easy as making it.
- **Do**: allow undo for 10 seconds after a transfer is scheduled.
- **Avoid**: making cancellation a phone call.

# Accessible by default
Accessibility is part of the first design, not a fix applied later. If someone cannot perceive or operate a screen, the screen is not finished.
- **Do**: meet a contrast ratio of 4.5 to 1.
- **Avoid**: relying on color alone for status.

# Calm, not urgent
Financial decisions deserve time. We give customers the facts and the deadline, and we let them decide at their own pace.
- **Do**: state plain deadlines.
- **Avoid**: countdown timers that push decisions.

# Explain the why
When we hold, block or decline something, customers deserve to know the reason. An explanation turns a frustrating dead end into a step they understand and can act on.
- **Do**: tell customers why a payment is on hold and what happens next.
- **Avoid**: showing "payment on hold" with no reason.

---

> If a customer has to read it twice, we wrote it wrong.
> — Ines Albrecht, Head of Design

! Disagreements with a principle go to the monthly design council, not to individual reviews.
